
PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT (PRD)
1) Project Overview
Project: Local web app that turns highlighted reading passages into high-quality spaced-repetition Q&A cards (Matuschak-style), lets me edit/curate them, and exports selected cards into Mochi Cards.
Working name: PromptForge
Version scope: V0 (text-only input, selection-based generation)

2) Purpose & Problem
Purpose
I want a fast workflow for turning what I’m reading into good retrieval practice prompts, not generic flashcards.
Problem it solves
Writing good spaced repetition prompts is hard and slow.
Most tools generate low-quality cards: vague, broad, yes/no, inconsistent, or too much per card.
I use Mochi already and want a quick pipeline into it.

3) Target User
Single user: Me
Usage mode: Local (personal machine)
Skill level: non-developer (but comfortable with Python basics)

4) Goals & Success Criteria
V0 goals
Paste text → highlight selection → click generate → receive 3–5 excellent Q&A cards.
Cards follow principles from Andy Matuschak’s prompt-writing guidance:
Focused (one knowledge unit)
Precise (unambiguous; avoids alternative correct answers)
Consistent (same answer each review)
Tractable (answerable reliably; not annoying)
Effortful (not guessable from the question wording alone)
Allow rapid editing and curation.
Export selected cards to Mochi in a format that imports cleanly.
Runs locally; saved work persists.
Non-goals (V0)
No screenshots/images (V1)
No full-document generation (selection-only)
No cloze deletions
No multiple-choice
No spaced repetition scheduling inside this app

5) Key Product Decisions (Locked)
Generation is selection-only (highlight text to generate).
Cards are Q&A only (no cloze).
Balanced card mix by default: factual + conceptual.
Difficulty slider exists: Basic / Medium / Deep.
No invented examples (cards must remain grounded in selected text).
Local-first: app runs locally; data and keys stored locally.
LLM providers: OpenAI + Anthropic (switchable; for testing).

6) Core User Flows
Flow A — Generate from selection
Open app → Workspace.
Paste reading material into editor.
Highlight a passage.
Choose difficulty (Basic/Medium/Deep).
Click Generate Cards (or keyboard shortcut).
App shows 3–5 generated cards.
User edits questions/answers.
User selects cards to keep and saves them to library.
Flow B — Save source text (optional)
After paste, app detects paste event.
Pop-up: “Save this source?” with:
Title (required to save)
Optional tags
User saves or dismisses.
Saved sources accessible later; cards can link to a source.
Flow C — Export to Mochi
User opens Saved Cards.
Filters/selects desired cards.
Click export:
CSV (Front/Back)
Markdown format
Downloads file to import into Mochi.
Flow D — Switch models/providers
User opens Settings.
Select provider (OpenAI/Anthropic).
Enter API key (stored locally).
Select model.
Click “Test” to verify.

7) Functional Requirements
7.1 Editor & Selection
Large text editor area for paste/edit.
Must support highlighting/selecting text inside the editor.
Show “Selection length” indicator (chars or words).
Generate Cards button disabled if selection is empty.
Optional: keyboard shortcut (Cmd/Ctrl+Enter) triggers generate if selection exists.
7.2 Card Generation (Selection-only)
Input to LLM:
Highlighted selection text
Difficulty setting
Optional source title
Output:
3–5 cards (or 1–2 if selection too small)
Each card must include question + answer and a short supporting quote
Card Types (metadata)
Cards must include a type:
factual (definitions, specific facts, named items, direct statements)
conceptual (purpose, distinctions, cause/effect, role/implication—only if supported)
procedural (steps/conditions—only if present in selection)
salience (rare; only in Deep when selection explicitly suggests a trigger/behavior)
7.3 Card Mix Rules (per your preference)
Default preference: mix factual + conceptual.
Basic
~70% factual, simple recall, minimal inference.
Medium (default)
~50/50 factual/conceptual when selection allows.
Deep
~70% conceptual (but still precise + consistent); allow max 1 salience card only if supported.
7.4 “No Examples” Rule (Strict)
Do not invent examples.
Do not ask user to generate examples (“Name 2 examples…”).
Only reference examples if explicitly present in the selection and needed to make a question precise.
7.5 Card Viewing, Editing, and Curation
Generated cards displayed in a list.
Each card has:
Editable Question
Editable Answer
Expand/collapse Source Quote
Checkbox: Keep
Actions:
Save kept cards to library
Delete generated cards
Regenerate (with same selection and settings)
7.6 Saved Cards Library
Separate view listing saved cards.
Filters:
Source title
Date created
Difficulty/provider/model (optional metadata)
Cards remain editable in library.
7.7 Export (Mochi)
Provide two exports:
CSV
Columns: Front, Back
Front = Question
Back = Answer
Markdown
Format per card:
Question
---
Answer
Cards separated by blank line.
Export only Q/A in V0 (no metadata in exports).

8) LLM Prompt Specification (V0)
8.1 Strict Output Schema
LLM must return JSON only, matching:
{
  "cards": [
    {
      "type": "factual|conceptual|procedural|salience",
      "question": "string",
      "answer": "string",
      "source_quote": "string",
      "inference": false
    }
  ]
}

Field rules
source_quote: exact excerpt from selection, max ~25 words.
inference: true only if answer is not explicitly stated.
Answers should be short and checkable (aim 3–20 words unless unavoidable).
8.2 Prompt Quality Rules (Matuschak-aligned)
Every card must satisfy:
Focused: one knowledge unit
Precise: no ambiguity, avoid alternative correct answers
Consistent: same answer each time
Tractable: realistically answerable
Effortful: cannot be answered by guessing from the prompt phrasing alone
Avoid yes/no questions.
Avoid pattern-matching long copied sentences; keep questions short.
8.3 Full Prompt Pack (copy/paste into code)
SYSTEM PROMPT
You are an expert at writing spaced-repetition retrieval practice prompts.
Your job is to produce high-quality Q&A flashcards that create durable understanding.
You follow these principles:
- Focused: one knowledge unit per card.
- Precise: unambiguous; avoid alternative correct answers.
- Consistent: same answer each time.
- Tractable: answerable reliably; not annoying.
- Effortful: requires retrieval, not guesswork.
Avoid yes/no questions. Avoid vague or overly broad prompts.
Return JSON only in the exact schema requested.

DEVELOPER PROMPT
TASK
Generate 3–5 spaced-repetition Q&A cards from ONLY the Selected Text provided by the user.
Cards must be in strict JSON format. No additional commentary.

HARD CONSTRAINTS
- Use ONLY the Selected Text. Do not use outside knowledge.
- Do NOT invent examples.
- Do NOT ask the user to generate examples.
- Do NOT create cloze deletions; Q&A only.
- Avoid yes/no questions.
- Each card must target ONE knowledge unit.
- Answers must be short and checkable (aim 3–20 words unless unavoidable).
- Include a short exact supporting quote (<= 25 words) from the Selected Text for each card.
- Set inference=false if the answer is explicitly supported by the quote; otherwise inference=true.

CARD MIX & DIFFICULTY
Default goal: produce a balanced set of factual + conceptual cards.

Difficulty = BASIC:
- Prefer factual cards (~70%).
- Simple direct questions.
- Avoid “why/how” unless directly stated in Selected Text.

Difficulty = MEDIUM:
- Balanced factual + conceptual (~50/50).
- Include conceptual edges when supported: purpose, difference, cause/effect.

Difficulty = DEEP:
- Prefer conceptual cards (~70%).
- Use why/how/role/implication only when directly supported.
- You may include at most 1 salience card only if Selected Text explicitly suggests a practical trigger/behavior.

NO EXAMPLES RULE (STRICT)
- Do NOT invent examples.
- Do NOT ask the user to generate examples.
- Only reference examples if explicitly present in Selected Text and required for precision.

OUTPUT FORMAT (STRICT JSON ONLY)
Return exactly:
{
  "cards": [
    {
      "type": "factual|conceptual|procedural|salience",
      "question": "...",
      "answer": "...",
      "source_quote": "...",
      "inference": false
    }
  ]
}

USER MESSAGE TEMPLATE
Title (optional): {TITLE}
Difficulty: {BASIC|MEDIUM|DEEP}

Selected Text:
"""
{SELECTED_TEXT}
"""


9) UI / Information Architecture
Tabs
Workspace
Editor (paste text)
Difficulty selector (Basic/Medium/Deep)
Generate Cards button
Generated Cards panel (edit + keep)
Saved Cards
Library list
Filters
Export buttons (CSV / Markdown)
Settings
Provider (OpenAI/Anthropic)
Model name
API key
Test connection
UX requirements
Clean, minimal, “slick” look.
Fast feedback: loading spinner + clear error message on failure.
Keyboard friendly.

10) Technical Requirements (Local-first)
10.1 Running locally
Web app runs locally in browser.
No server required for V0 (unless needed for key security; but requirement is local-first).
10.2 Storage
Persist locally:
Saved sources (optional)
Saved cards
Settings (provider/model)
API keys (locally; with warning)
Recommended:
IndexedDB for cards/sources
localStorage for lightweight settings (ok)
10.3 Provider support
Must support both:
OpenAI API
Anthropic API
Provider switch changes endpoint + request shape accordingly.
10.4 Limits & safety
Selection max length (configurable, e.g. 10k–20k chars).
If selection too long:
warn user
offer truncate option.
10.5 Robust JSON handling
Validate returned JSON.
If invalid JSON:
auto-retry once with “Return valid JSON in schema” repair instruction
if still invalid: show raw output + copy button.

11) Data Model
Source
id
title
content
created_at
tags (optional)
Card
id
source_id (nullable)
type
question
answer
source_quote
inference
created_at
provider/model (optional metadata)

12) Acceptance Criteria (V0)
✅ Generate works only with a selection.
✅ Output is 3–5 Q&A cards, strict JSON schema, with quotes.
✅ Cards are focused/precise/consistent/tractable/effortful.
✅ Difficulty slider changes card depth and mix correctly.
✅ No invented examples appear.
✅ Edit + keep + saved library works.
✅ Export to CSV/Markdown works for Mochi import.
✅ Local persistence across refresh/reopen.
✅ Provider switching (OpenAI/Anthropic) works and is testable.

13) Future Version (V1)
Add screenshot/image input:
upload screenshot
OCR or vision extraction
then selection-based generation on extracted text
Potential later:
prompt revision workflow (flag weak cards)
batch generation across multiple selections


